 2. Exclusive: Enter Ibex 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 The Ibex autonomous ground vehicle. Photo: Courtesy of Overland AI

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Overland AI this morning will debut Ibex, an autonomous ground vehicle capable of shuttling thousands of pounds of materiel and designed for rapid relocation aboard V-22 Ospreys.

 Why it matters: The Seattle startup is building the reconfigurable robo-resupplier for the Marine Corps as well as the Army and Special Operations Command.

 The big picture: Ibex has been in design since the beginning of the year, Overland AI co-founder Greg Okopal told me. Two prototypes are undergoing daily tests in central Washington. Some tweaks and refinements are expected ahead of production later this year.

 More than a dozen AGVs are on order, via a nearly $20 million APFIT contract with the Marine Corps ground-based air-defense shop and a previously unreported $4.5 million add-on that brings in the 75th Ranger Regiment. "We are a very field-forward, field-testing-focused company," Okopal said. "If you're going to deliver these vehicles to a unit, it needs to keep up with what they are doing," he said. "It cannot slow them down." Zoom in: Ibex is based on a Polaris MRZR Alpha , a platform also tapped for the Corp's Ultra-Light Tactical Vehicle. It can carry 2,000 pounds, hit speeds greater than 35 mph and cruise for 250 miles. It's fueled by diesel or JP-8.

 "It's narrower, it's longer, but otherwise pretty close in scale to what an Ultra is," Okopal said, referencing the startup's other AGV. "It's a lot of payload space, a lot of payload capacity," he added. "You can add sensors. You can add effectors." Context: Ibex will initially be used to restock Marine Air Defense Integrated Systems, which are used for counter-drone and overhead defense. Overland AI teased that application over the summer — but kept the actual vehicle under wraps.

 The bottom line: "We can use roads, if they're there. But we do not need roads. We can move through the most challenging terrain that is out there," Okopal said.

 "And we go fast." Go deeper: Army creates autonomy command amid Hegseth's robo-warfare push 

 
 
