 3. Siri but deadly 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Soldiers conduct live-fire training with an M2 Bradley at Fort Irwin, California, in 2022. Photo: Hunter Xue/DVIDS

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Near-future versions of General Dynamics' next-generation tank and XM30 combat vehicle could have voice controls onboard, letting crews manipulate sensors, radios, drones and more with plain English instructions.

 Why it matters: Such a setup could simplify training, ease troop burdens and improve battlefield performance — or what the military so coyly calls lethality.

 Driving the news: General Dynamics Land Systems and Primordial Labs today announced a "master teaming agreement," after years of working together.

 The former makes heavy-duty, household-name vehicles, like the Abrams and Stryker. The latter makes a locally operating human-machine interface known as Anura. I gave Anura a go early last year, orchestrating small drones in a field in Virginia . What they're saying: "I'm tracking that this is a first-of-its-kind effort to put this on ground combat vehicles in the U.S. Army," GDLS director of strategy Geoff Norman, the former head of the Next Generation Combat Vehicles Cross-Functional Team, told me.

 "We do think this is going to result in more-rapid crew functions, inside the vehicle." Zoom in: Anura-enabled voice controls are anticipated for future upgrade packages, according to Norman . The idea, he said, "is to spin the capability into future model years, as we really gain the insights from soldier experiments and soldier feedback."

 Zoom out: This is a perfect example of big working with little, and vice versa.

 General Dynamics is the third-largest defense contractor in the world by revenue. Primordial disclosed a seed round, led by Squadra Ventures, in 2023. "We're along for the ride on multiple GDLS platforms," Primordial CEO Lee Ritholtz told me. Go deeper: Army taps Palantir for 8 AI-fueled TITAN trucks 

 
 
