 4. Golden questions 
 
 By Thomas Novelly 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Natalie Peeples/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Top senators are questioning the effectiveness and cost of President Trump's Golden Dome missile defense effort. 

 Why it matters: Trump's sprawling plan is still failing to win needed support from lawmakers — and it's in dire financial straits.

 State of play: Lawmakers and defense analysts said during a Sept. 29 Senate Armed Services subcommittee hearing they're in the dark about Golden Dome's technology and capabilities.

 Sen. Angus King (I-Maine) said given various cost estimates it appeared that nationwide missile coverage was "not in the cards" and wondered "who is protected and who isn't?" A spokesperson for the Defense Department's Golden Dome office declined to comment. By the numbers: In May, the Congressional Budget Office estimated the missile shield could cost as much as $1.2 trillion over 20 years.

 Sen. Deb Fischer (R-Neb.) told a CBO official during last week's hearing she wanted "more informed analysis in the future." Yes, but: David Mosher, CBO's national security analysis director, told lawmakers CBO received its first briefing from the Golden Dome office just five days before the Sept. 29 hearing.

 Gen. Michael Guetlein, Golden Dome's program manager, has decried the CBO estimate and said the system will remain within a $185 billion budget. Follow the money: Golden Dome's been funded exclusively through reconciliation, a maneuver that allows the Republican majority to pass additional spending.

 It netted $24.4 billion last year, and almost all of the $17.5 billion requested for the program this year comes from reconciliation. 
 
